= Inference <inference>

The type equations unification solves are generated by the algorithmic system that reconstructs types for a program. The inference algorithm follows algorithm W in that it walks an expression, draws fresh variables and solves the resulting equations by unification. It departs from algorithm W at selection. When a lookup reaches a row-variable, shadowing is unclear and the lookup cannot proceed. Instead of guessing the outcome, the algorithm delays the decision: it returns a placeholder $δ$ and _parks_ the lookup in the solver state. Every such _parked stump_ is filed under its _blocker_, a row-variable. When unification solves the blocker, the stump _wakes up_ and the lookup advances further into the row. A hit or a miss determines $δ$, and a later row-variable blocks the stump again and _reparks_ it there. At let-generalization, the stumps blocked on generalized variables move into the scheme as _qualifiers_ that are replayed at every instance.

For example, in $r: (x: x.l) (r ‖ {m = c})$ the selection $x.l$ parks a stump on the row $β$ of $x$. The application then solves $β ≔ (m: 𝓫_c | β_r)$, which wakes the stump. Its lookup skips $m$ and stops at the row $β_r$ of $r$, where the stump is parked again. The answer keeps it as a qualifier, and a use that leaves its lookup undecided reads it as ★.

== Solver State

#let solver_state = figure(
  caption: "The solver state.",
  $
     #type_name("Solver state") S & ::= (θ, Δ, K) \
    #type_name("Parked stumps") Δ & ::= ∅ | ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩, Δ \
  $,
)
#solver_state <solver-state>

The inference judgement $Γ; S ⊢ e ⇒ τ; S′$ takes a _solver state_ $S$ and returns the updated state $S′$. The solver state $S$, given in @solver-state, consists of a sort-respecting substitution $θ$, a list $Δ$ of parked stumps and a map $K$ of the sorts at which each name was drawn. $⟦S⟧τ$ applies the substitution of $S$ to $τ$, $S(α)$ reads the sort of $α$ from $K$, and $Δ_i$ denotes the parked stumps of $S_i$. $S #st_park q$ parks a stump, and $S #st_drop Δ′$ drops stumps.

A _parked stump_ $⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩$ is a lookup of $l$ in $ρ$ with result variable $δ$ and blocker $α$. We call a stump _stale_ when a substitution has solved its _recorded blocker_ ($α$ is outdated) and this happens when unification solves type variables. A parked stump leaves the fresh type-variable $δ$ in its place, and unification may solve $δ$ while the stump is parked. We call a stump whose result variable $δ$ was used by unification a _spent stump_, but only when $δ$ has been solved to a type that is neither a variable nor ★.

The state is kept under one invariant, _quiescence_. Quiescence demands that every stump in $Δ$ is genuinely blocked on its recorded variable $α$. Every written solution can break this condition, so an auxiliary judgement re-runs the stale lookups, a process we call _saturation_. Saturation uses _wake-up_ as a subroutine, a judgement that re-runs the lookup on stumps.

#example(name: [Stale stumps and saturation])[
  In $r: (x: x.l) (r ‖ {m = c})$, the selection parks $⟨β ⊳ β.l ↓ δ⟩$ with recorded blocker $β$. The application solves $β ≔ (m: 𝓫_c | β_r)$, and the stump becomes stale: its lookup is no longer blocked on $β$. Saturation wakes it, the lookup skips $m$ and blocks on $β_r$, and the stump is parked again under $β_r$. No stump is stale afterwards, so the state is quiescent. The result $δ$ is never solved, so the stump is not spent.
]

== Wake-Up and Saturation

#let wakeup = figure(
  caption: "Waking and saturating stumps.",
  box(width: 100%, stack(
    spacing: 18pt,
    subbox(caption: "Wake One Stump")[
      #flexbox(
        derive(
          "K-hit",
          ($(⟦S⟧ρ).l ↓ τ$, $S ⊢ δ ≐ τ ⇝ S′$),
          $S ⊢ ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ #st_wake S′ #st_drop ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩$,
        ),
        derive(
          "K-⊥",
          ($(⟦S⟧ρ).l ↓ ⊥$, $S ⊢ δ ≐ ★ ⇝ S′$),
          $S ⊢ ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ #st_wake S′ #st_drop ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩$,
        ),
        derive(
          "K-repark",
          ($(⟦S⟧ρ).l ↓ #h(0.2em) ? "on" α′$,),
          $S ⊢ ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ #st_wake (S #st_drop ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩) #st_park ⟨α′ ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩$,
        ),
      )
    ],
    subbox(caption: "Saturate")[
      #flexbox(
        derive("K!-done", ($S "quiescent"$,), $S ⊢ Δ #st_sat S$),
        derive(
          "K!-step",
          (
            $⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ ∈ Δ$,
            $¬(⟦S⟧ρ).l ↓ #h(0.2em) ? "on" α$,
            $S ⊢ ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ #st_wake S₁$,
            $S₁ ⊢ Δ₁ #st_sat S′$,
          ),
          $S ⊢ Δ #st_sat S′$,
        ),
      )
    ],
  )),
)
#wakeup <wakeup>

@wakeup gives the judgements for wake-ups and saturation. The judgement $S ⊢ ⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩ #st_wake S′$ wakes a single stump and re-checks the lookup. The three rules match the three verdicts of a lookup. K-hit applies when the lookup hits a definite type: it drops the stump and unifies $δ$ with the found type. K-⊥ drops the stump and pins $δ$ to ★. K-repark moves the stump to the new blocker $α′$. The stability lemma of @lookup-metatheory makes this sound because a definite lookup never changes, even after further substitutions.

The saturation judgement $#st_sat$ works on the state until it is quiescent again: it picks a stale stump from $Δ$ and runs wake-up $#st_wake$ on it. The first two premises of K!-step select a stump that is parked whose lookup is no longer blocked on the recorded blocker $α$. The lookup may still block on another variable, and then K-repark applies. The third premise wakes the stump, and the fourth continues on the new state. K!-done ends a run when no more stale stumps exist.

