#inference <inference-rules>

@inference-rules gives the syntax-directed version of the declarative rules from @declarative-rules. T-★-intro has no counterpart, since the algorithm never discards a type and T-eq has none either, since row unification already works up to $≈$. T-let is discussed in @let-generalization. A-cons and A-λ-I are standard. A-rec and the A-ξ rules thread the state through a record body and build its row. A-λ-E and A-conc draw fresh variables for the shape their premises demand and give the resulting equations to $#st_solve$. The judgement $S ⊢ τ ≐ τ' ⇝ S'$ runs the unification algorithm of @unification and writes its solution into the state and $S ⊢ τ ≐ τ′ #st_solve S′$ does the same and _saturates_ afterwards.

A-var instantiates a qualified scheme with fresh variables at the sorts of its binders and replays the instantiated stumps by $scripts(↝)^(*!)$. A scheme stump carries no blocker, so the replay $scripts(↝)^*$ computes one: it answers the stumps that the instance already decides and parks the others under the variables that block them. The definition for this judgement is deferred to @app-replay. Saturation $#st_sat$ follows the replay, since a replayed stump whose result is already solved can write a row solution and make a stump stale that the replay has already parked.

As in @declarative-rules, three selection rules cover the three verdicts of the lookup judgement. Each rule infers a type for $e$, unifies it with ${β}$ for a fresh row-variable $β$ and runs the lookup on the solved row $⟦S₂⟧β$. A-sel and A-sel-⊥ use the verdict directly but A-sel-\? is where the stump mechanism enters. Its premise $(⟦S₂⟧β).l ↓ #h(0.2em) ? "on" α$ states that the lookup answers $?$ and names its blocker $α$. The rule does not commit ★, but returns a fresh type-variable $δ$ as a placeholder for the result and parks the stump under $α$.

== Let-Generalization <let-generalization>

#let generalization = figure(
  caption: "Generalization.",
  stack(
    spacing: 14pt,
    flexbox(
      derive(
        "A-let",
        stack(
          spacing: 8pt,
          $Γ; S₁ ⊢ e₁ ⇒ τ₁; S₂ #h(2em) macron(α) = "gen"_(Γ, S₁)(S₂, τ₁)$,
          $Γ · (x: ∀(macron(α): S₂(macron(α))). ⟦S₂⟧Δ_q ⇒ ⟦S₂⟧τ₁); S₂ #st_drop Δ_q ⊢ e₂ ⇒ τ₂; S₃$,
        ),
        $Γ; S₁ ⊢ #b[let] x = e₁ #b[in] e₂ ⇒ τ₂; S₃$,
      ),
    ),
    align(
      left,
      $
        Δ_q &= {p ∈ Δ₂ | "blocker"(p) ∈ macron(α)} #h(3em) Δ_Γ = Δ₂ #st_drop Δ_q \
        "gen"_(Γ, S₁)(S₂, τ₁) &= "the greatest" macron(α) ⊆ "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧τ₁, ⟦S₂⟧Δ₂) "such that" \
        & #h(1em) macron(α) ∩ "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧(Γ, Δ_Γ)) = ∅ \
        & #h(1em) "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧"results"(Δ_q)) ⊆ macron(α) \
        & #h(1em) Δ_q ∩ Δ₁ = ∅ \
      $,
    ),
  ),
)
#generalization <generalization>

@generalization gives A-let. The rule infers $e₁$, binds the scheme and continues with $e₂$. The choice of the generalized variables $macron(α)$ is delegated to the function $"gen"_(Γ, S₁)$ that partitions the stumps into two groups. $Δ_q$ is the list of stumps blocked on a variable of the scheme, and these stumps go into the scheme as its qualifiers $Q$, read as constraints without their blockers. $Δ_Γ = Δ₂ #st_drop Δ_q$ are the remaining stumps, which stay in the solver state. The partition relies on quiescence because otherwise, a stump might be blocked on a solved variable and not on one of $macron(α)$. It would then stay in $Δ_Γ$ and the result variable would not be generalized.

The three lines in $"gen"_(Γ, S₁)$ make a choice of $macron(α)$ admissible. The first condition ensures that all variables are fresh for the environment which consists of $Γ$ together with the stumps that stay parked. It is the same condition as in Hindley-Milner generalization @damas_milner. The second condition makes sure that all stump results ($δ$) are themselves generalized as they are part of the stumps. The last condition ensures that $Δ_q$ only contains stumps parked while inferring $e₁$ because a stump parked before $e₁$ would otherwise never be instantiated.

The algorithm computes the set of variables by deleting those that don't hold the statements so a failing condition never makes the binding completely monomorphic but only parts of it.

#example(name: [Generalizing a selection])[
  Consider $#b[let] f = x: x.l #b[in] f {l = c}$. For the bound term, A-λ-I gives $x$ a fresh $γ$, and A-sel-\? unifies $γ$ with ${β}$ for a fresh $β$, finds that $β.l$ answers $?$ on $β$, and parks $⟨β ⊳ β.l ↓ δ⟩$ with a fresh $δ$. The bound term has type ${β} → δ$. Since $γ$ is solved, $macron(α) = β, δ$. The single stump moves into the scheme, and nothing stays parked. $f$ is bound to $∀(β: "Row", δ: "Type"). ⟨β.l ↓ δ⟩ ⇒ {β} → δ$, which is $"selQ"$ of @principality. At the use, A-var instantiates the scheme with fresh $β′$ and $δ′$ and replays the stump, which is still blocked and is parked on $β′$. A-λ-E then solves $β′ ≔ (l: 𝓫_c)$ and identifies $δ′$ with the result variable. Saturation finds the stump stale, K-hit re-asks the lookup, which now hits, and the result is unified with $𝓫_c$.
] <ex-let-selection>


== Entry Point

#let entry = figure(
  caption: "The entry point.",
  stack(
    spacing: 14pt,
    flexbox(
      derive(
        "Entry",
        (
          $∅; (∅, ∅, ∅) ⊢ e ⇒ τ; S₁$,
          $S₁ ⊢ Δ₁ #st_merges S₂$,
          $S₂ "fillable"$,
        ),
        $⊢ e ⇒ σ$,
      ),
    ),
    align(
      left,
      $σ = ∀(macron(α): S₁(macron(α))). ⟦S₁⟧Δ₁ ⇒ ⟦S₁⟧τ #h(3em) macron(α) = "ftv"(⟦S₁⟧τ, ⟦S₁⟧Δ₁)$,
    ),
  ),
)
#entry <entry>

The entry point is given in @entry. It runs inference from the empty state and returns a top-level scheme with all its stumps attached, similar to A-let. For soundness, the entry point must check that $σ$ has an instance (@thm-infer-sound), and we use a sufficient syntactic check for this condition. A spent stump already has a result assigned to it that is neither a variable nor ★. The lookup must then hit and the instance must put a field into its blocker that has the needed type. The fillability test checks whether this is possible by building one instance and checking two conditions on it. It replaces every type-variable with ★ and adds the fields fixed by spent stumps in front of their blockers. This way, every spent stump gets the result it expects.

Two things can go wrong in this assignment. Given a spent stump $⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩$, every other stump with the same blocker and lookup label must be spent as well, and the results have to coincide. The reasoning is that the blocker provides the label in one case so it also has to answer it for the other case. Since it is the same blocker variable, it has to be the same result. Second, a field type can mention another blocker, so the blockers have to be filled in order, and a cycle admits no order. We write $β ≻ β′$ when a spent stump on $β$ has $β′$, the blocker of a spent stump, in its result. In $x: (x.l) x$, the $l$-stump on the row $ρ$ of $x$ is spent at ${ρ} → γ$, so $ρ ≻ ρ$. The row $ρ$ would then contain a field whose type contains $ρ$ itself, which needs a recursive type.

